window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][76] = "<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> analytics · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C14 · <strong>Competency:</strong> AN · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> In an A/B test, time spent per user is up 6% and revenue per user is down 3%. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Time spent is up, so ship,&quot; or &quot;revenue is down, so don't.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks which metric reflects the goal and whether each result is real.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Checks validity and intervals for both, uses the pre-set metric hierarchy, converts the revenue effect into dollars, investigates the mechanism and segments, adds a long-term holdout, and decides by a rule.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Validity first.</em> Check the split of users, the duration (full weekly cycles) and any novelty effect. Then read both results with intervals. Suppose 200,000 users per arm (invented). Time per user rose from 30.0 to 31.8 minutes (+1.8, a 6% gain); with a standard deviation of about 40 minutes the standard error of the difference is about 0.13, so the interval is roughly +1.55 to +2.05: clearly positive. Revenue per user fell from $2.00 to $1.94 (−$0.06); with a standard deviation of about $9 the standard error is about $0.028, giving an interval from about −$0.116 to −$0.004: a small but real fall, near the edge of detectability. <em>Which metric is the goal?</em> The test should have had a primary metric and guardrails written in advance (FW-C14-05). If the feature exists to raise engagement and revenue is a guardrail, a statistically real fall in the guardrail has to be explained, not waved away; if the product's business depends on revenue per user, revenue is the primary and time spent is a mechanism. <em>Convert to dollars.</em> With 5 million monthly users, −$0.06 per user is −$300,000 a month, or $3.6 million a year. The question is whether the extra 1.8 minutes buys something worth more: higher retention, more ad inventory or more purchases later. That is a hypothesis, not a result. <em>Find the mechanism.</em> Why would time rise and revenue fall? Perhaps the feature displaces purchase flows or ad slots (a mechanical effect), or it brings in light users who spend less (a mix effect), or it delays checkout. Break the result down by segment, by surface and by funnel step. If it is mix, the effect on retained revenue may be positive over time; if it is displacement, the feature may be trading revenue for minutes. <em>Check the long term.</em> Keep a long-term holdout and compare 90-day retention and revenue. Time spent is not value by itself; if retention does not move, the revenue loss is simply a cost. <em>Decision.</em> Follow the pre-set rule. If revenue was a guardrail with a margin tighter than the observed fall, do not ship as is; redesign to protect the purchase flow, or ship to the segments where revenue is not hurt, and run the long-term check. If there was no rule, set one now and say that it was a gap. What would change my view: evidence that retention and later revenue rise enough to repay $3.6 million a year. <em>Signals.</em> Both metrics read with intervals; the metric hierarchy; dollars; mechanism and mix; long-term holdout; a rule.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c15-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C15-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C15 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> How do you prioritize features?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I use RICE&quot; (or another framework), with nothing more.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Starts from the goal, derives criteria, sizes options and decides.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats frameworks as tools, runs a sensitivity check, names the opportunity cost and the riskiest assumption, and says how stakeholders are brought along.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I start with the goal, because the same list ranks differently under different goals. I ask what we are trying to move this quarter and what is fixed: capacity, the date, dependencies. Then I derive criteria from the goal: who must be affected, by how much on the goal metric, how sure we are, and what it costs. If the goal is new-user activation, reach means new users, not everyone. Then I size each option with a reason and a range, and I use a framework such as RICE only to organize the numbers: reach, impact, confidence, effort. I'd say where each number comes from and what the least certain one is. I rerun the ranking with that number changed; if the top option holds, I trust it, and if two options swap, I know that is where to spend discussion or a small test. I name the opportunity cost: choosing A means not building B, and I say what B was worth. I look for the riskiest assumption in the winner and the cheapest way to test it before a full build. I decide, say what we are not doing and when we'd revisit it, and I take the reasoning to stakeholders so a &quot;not now&quot; is understood, not imposed. The mistake I'd avoid is letting a score be the decision. A framework gives a way to talk about the options; the decision is a judgment about the goal, the evidence and the tradeoffs.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c15-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C15-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C15 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> In a scoping meeting, every stakeholder says their items are &quot;must-haves.&quot; How do you handle it?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd get everyone to agree&quot; or &quot;I'd decide myself.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Uses a tool such as MoSCoW with a constraint to force tradeoffs.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Anchors &quot;must&quot; to the goal and the date, uses capacity to test the list, makes tradeoffs visible, and leaves the decision with a named owner.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> When everything is a must, the word has lost its meaning, so I'd redefine it against a constraint. A &quot;must&quot; is something without which the release fails its goal or cannot ship at the date (a legal requirement, a core flow). I'd restate the goal and the date, then ask of each item: &quot;If this were missing at launch, would the release still deliver its goal?&quot; Then I'd bring capacity into the room. Suppose the &quot;musts&quot; total 40 person-weeks and we have 24 with a buffer. I'd say: &quot;These can't all be musts; we have capacity for 24. What leaves?&quot; Putting the number in front of people shifts the conversation from defending items to choosing among them. I'd use MoSCoW (must, should, could, won't this time) to sort, with the &quot;won't this time&quot; column given as much attention as &quot;must,&quot; because it shows people the item is deferred and not dismissed, with a date. For disputes I'd offer options with costs rather than argue: &quot;If we keep A, we lose C and D; if we keep C and D, A moves to the next release. Which serves the goal?&quot; The decision belongs to a named owner, usually me with the lead engineer and designer, with the sponsor informed. After the meeting, I'd send the written list with the reasons, so the criteria are on record. What would change my mind: new evidence that a deferred item is a blocker for the goal.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c15-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C15-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C15 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Three candidates for the next system release: better notifications, a new widget, a battery mode. Pick one.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The battery mode, because people care about battery.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> States a goal, derives criteria, compares the three on them, and picks one with a reason.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Shows the ranking depends on the goal, runs a sensitivity check, names tradeoffs (including effects on developers, privacy and other devices), tests the riskiest assumption, and says what would change the choice.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I know the product only from public information; nothing here describes any real plan. I'd first ask what the release is meant to achieve, because the right pick depends on it. Assume the goal is to raise day-to-day satisfaction for the largest group of users across a range of devices, with a secondary aim of keeping developers engaged. I'd say this is my assumption and ask whether it is right. <em>Criteria from the goal.</em> How many users benefit, how much it improves their day (impact), how sure we are, how much it costs to build, and what it does to others: developers (a widget depends on them), the user's other devices, privacy, and the platform's consistency. <em>Sizing (invented).</em> Reach as a share of users, impact on a 0.25 to 3 scale, confidence, and effort in person-months. | Option | Reach | Impact | Confidence | Effort | Score | |---|---|---|---|---|---| | Battery mode | 60% | 2 | 70% | 4 | 21.0 | | Better notifications | 80% | 1 | 50% | 8 | 5.0 | | New widget | 40% | 1 | 60% | 5 | 4.8 | <em>Reasoning behind the numbers.</em> Battery mode reaches the people who run low in a day (most mobile users sometimes), has a measurable benefit (hours of use) and is a setting, which is lower risk to existing behavior. Notifications touch nearly everyone, but a better version is hard to define, so confidence is low, effort is high, and changes affect developers and the experience of users who like theirs (the legacy-user question). A widget depends on developers adopting it, so its reach is limited by their uptake. <em>Sensitivity.</em> The battery mode's impact is the key number. If it is 1 instead of 2, its score is 10.5, still more than twice the others. For the notifications to win, their confidence and effort would both have to be far better than assumed. So the choice is robust to this uncertainty. It would change if the goal changed: if the aim were to strengthen the developer ecosystem, the widget would rise, since it creates something for developers to build. <em>Riskiest assumption and test.</em> That users would turn on a battery mode and accept its tradeoffs (reduced performance or background activity). A cheap test is a prototype setting with a small group measuring opt-in and satisfaction, with a guardrail on complaints about slowness. I'd also check the interaction with other devices (does it sync across a user's devices?). <em>Decision.</em> &quot;I'd pick the battery mode: it serves the most users with the clearest benefit for the least cost and risk. I'd defer notifications until we can define the improvement, and the widget until developer demand is clearer. What would change my mind: evidence that the benefit is under half what I assumed, or a goal that favors the ecosystem.&quot; <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that prioritization prompts at Apple involve choosing among candidate features for a release <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any real release, plan or user research.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c15-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C15-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C15 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Microsoft</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Three large enterprise customers each want a different thing: admin controls, better performance, more integrations. They are 12%, 9% and 6% of revenue. How do you prioritize?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Do the biggest customer's request first.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Separates revenue at risk from breadth of need and effort, and looks beyond the three.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes blockers (compliance) from preferences, finds shared needs, sizes with a framework and sensitivity, protects against concentration and precedent, and plans communication.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd avoid ranking by customer size, because it makes the roadmap a function of who shouts loudest and ignores the other 197 customers. My approach: separate what each request is <em>for</em>, how broad the need is, and what it costs. <em>Understand the needs.</em> For each customer, ask what outcome they want and what happens if they do not get it. The largest customer's admin controls might be a compliance requirement (a blocker, with a deadline) or a convenience. If it is a compliance blocker, it is a constraint: either we meet it or we risk the account; I'd then look for the smallest set of controls that satisfies the requirement. <em>Breadth.</em> Check the wider base. Suppose (invented) of 200 customers, 30 have asked for admin controls, 55 for performance and 80 for integrations. | Option | Customers (reach) | Impact | Confidence | Effort (pm) | Score | |---|---|---|---|---|---| | Integrations | 80 | 1 | 70% | 4 | 14.0 | | Performance | 55 | 2 | 60% | 9 | 7.3 | | Admin controls | 30 | 1 | 80% | 6 | 4.0 | Integrations score highest because of breadth and low effort. Performance has the highest impact per user but costs the most. Admin controls reach the fewest customers, but that count is not the whole story: the largest customer sits there. <em>Overlay revenue and risk.</em> The 12% customer is $X of revenue; if the need is a blocker, the expected loss from refusing (probability of leaving times revenue) may exceed the value of the next item, as in the break-even arithmetic in Worked example 5. I'd talk to them to learn the probability and the minimum viable set. <em>Decision.</em> Deliver the minimum compliance subset of admin controls first if it is a true blocker (perhaps 2.5 person-months), then integrations, then performance in stages, giving each customer a dated answer. I'd avoid a custom build for any one customer and prefer general features. I'd also address concentration: three customers are 27% of revenue, which is a business risk to reduce by serving the broader base. <em>Communicate.</em> Share the criteria and the order with all three, with what they get and when, and what the alternative is until then. What would change my mind: learning that the 9% customer's performance issue is causing active evaluation of a competitor. <em>Company note.</em> Microsoft is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that enterprise-prioritization prompts at Microsoft separate buyer and user needs <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Microsoft data or product behavior is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c15-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C15-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C15 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Meta</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Two teams want the same engineers for the next quarter: the growth team, to run experiments to raise new-user engagement, and the integrity team, to reduce harmful content. How do you decide?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Whichever has the bigger numbers.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Sees that the goals are not in one unit, defines criteria that apply to both, and proposes an allocation.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates constraints (a minimum level of safety) from tradeable goals, uses guardrails and thresholds, sets a review date, and handles the politics with a decision rule and an escalation path.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I know the product only from public information. The first thing I'd notice is that the two goals are not in the same unit: growth is measured in engagement, integrity in the prevalence of harmful content. Trading them with one score would be false precision. So I'd separate the question into what is a constraint and what is a choice. <em>Constraints first.</em> Some integrity work is not tradeable: legal requirements, commitments to regulators, and a minimum safety standard the company has set. I'd ask the integrity team which of their items are in that category. Those are funded first, as constraints, and the growth team cannot trade against them. <em>What remains is a choice.</em> For the rest, I'd define criteria that apply to both: contribution to the company's goals for the period, size of the expected effect with a range, confidence, cost of delay (what gets worse if we wait), and risk (including reputational and user harm). Cost of delay is important for integrity: a harm that grows each week is different from an experiment that can wait. <em>Sizing (invented).</em> Say there are 6 engineers. Growth proposes experiments with an expected +0.8% in new-user engagement, a 40% chance each of a positive result. Integrity proposes work to reduce harmful content views from 0.12% to 0.08% of views, a one-third reduction, with higher confidence. These cannot be compared in units, but each can be checked against a threshold the company has set (for example, a target prevalence). <em>Allocation.</em> Not all-or-nothing. A sensible split: fund integrity to the level that meets the constraint and the target, say 3 engineers, and give 3 to growth, with a guardrail on the growth work (no experiment ships that raises harm metrics beyond a set margin). If the integrity target is not met at 3, move more. Review at the midpoint of the quarter with data. <em>Decision rule and escalation.</em> If the two leads cannot agree, decide by a rule agreed in advance: constraints first, then expected contribution to company goals weighted by cost of delay. If they still disagree, escalate to the shared leader with a one-page tradeoff table, not a debate. <em>Why this is not a compromise by default.</em> A 50-50 split has no logic; the split follows from the constraint and the target. What would change my mind: new evidence that the harm is growing fast, which would move engineers to integrity, or a growth opportunity that expires. <em>Company note.</em> Meta is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that prioritization prompts at Meta can set growth and integrity goals against each other <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Meta data or internal practice is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c15-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C15-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C15 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Option A is a sure, modest win. Option B is a big win that might not work. You have capacity for one. How do you choose?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;B has the bigger upside, so B,&quot; or &quot;A is safe, so A.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Compares expected values per unit of effort and considers risk appetite.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes expected value net of cost, shows how a cheap test changes the comparison, treats the probabilities as assumptions, and sequences the options.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd compare expected value net of cost, then see whether a test changes the picture. Suppose (invented) A is a $2,000,000 annual benefit with a 60% chance of working, costing 4 person-months at $25,000 each ($100,000). B is a $5,000,000 benefit with a 20% chance, costing 10 person-months ($250,000). - A: 0.6 × $2,000,000 = $1,200,000, less $100,000, is $1,100,000. - B: 0.2 × $5,000,000 = $1,000,000, less $250,000, is $750,000. On expected value, A is ahead even though B's prize is bigger. Per person-month, A returns about $300,000 and B about $100,000. <em>Does a test change B?</em> The 20% chance is an assumption. Suppose a concierge or prototype test costs 1 person-month ($25,000) and reliably tells us whether B is viable. If we build B only when the test says yes (20% of the time), the expected value is 0.2 × $5,000,000 less 0.2 × $250,000 for the build, less $25,000 for the test: $1,000,000 − $50,000 − $25,000 = $925,000. The test improves B from $750,000 to $925,000 by avoiding the 80% of builds that would fail. B-with-test is still behind A's $1,100,000. <em>Decision.</em> Do A first. Run the cheap test of B in parallel if there is any spare capacity, because the test is worth more than it costs. The choice depends on the probabilities, so I'd state them as assumptions and invite challenge: for B to beat A, its chance of working would need to be above about 27% ($1,100,000 + $250,000 = $1,350,000, divided by $5,000,000 is 27%, ignoring the test). <em>Beyond the numbers.</em> Risk appetite matters. If the company needs a big bet (a strategic gap, a competitor), B's upside may matter more than expected value, and a portfolio view (several A's and a B) is better than choosing one. And the learning from testing B has option value. What would change my mind: evidence that A's 60% is overstated, or that B's market window is closing.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c15-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C15-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C15 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A senior executive wants you to build their idea next. The data suggests another item is better. How do you handle it?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Do what the executive says,&quot; or &quot;refuse and cite the data.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Treats it as a decision with evidence, brings options and a recommendation, and uses a respectful script.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Seeks the need behind the idea, separates what the data shows from what it cannot, proposes a cheap test, offers a way to include the idea, and keeps the relationship and the decision rights clear.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd begin by assuming the executive knows something I don't: a customer conversation, a strategic bet, a competitor's move. So the first step is to ask about the need behind the idea, not to defend my list. &quot;Help me understand what you're seeing that makes this the priority.&quot; Often the idea is a solution to a problem that can be solved in more than one way. <em>Show the tradeoff, not just my view.</em> I'd bring the options with their costs. Say the executive's idea is X and my data points to Y. A one-page table: expected impact on the agreed goal with a range, effort, confidence, and what each displaces. &quot;If we build X, we defer Y, which we estimate at $A a year; X's value depends on assumption Z, which we have not tested.&quot; <em>Be honest about the data's limits.</em> Data on past behavior does not capture a new strategic bet. If the executive's case is strategic, my numbers on near-term impact are not the whole argument. I'd say what the data does and does not show. <em>Offer routes that respect the executive.</em> (1) A cheap test of X's riskiest assumption in two weeks, with success and kill criteria agreed beforehand; (2) a smaller version of X that fits alongside Y; (3) a sequence, Y now and X next quarter with a date. These let the executive see that their idea was taken seriously. <em>Use the script (FW-C15-07).</em> &quot;I see the need: [need]. To build X now we'd delay Y, which means [consequence]. What I can do is [test or smaller version]. If the test shows [result], I'd move X up.&quot; And keep the decision rights clear: if they decide to proceed after seeing the tradeoff, I'd do it well and record the assumption so we learn from the outcome, without saying &quot;I told you so.&quot; <em>What I'd avoid.</em> Hiding the tradeoff to avoid conflict, quietly deprioritizing, or turning it into a contest of status. What would change my mind: the executive revealing a fact (a commitment, a customer deadline) that changes the value of X.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c15-08\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C15-08</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C15 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A feature used by 4% of weekly users takes about one and a half engineers to maintain. Should you retire it?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, it's a small share,&quot; or &quot;no, someone uses it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Looks at who uses it, the cost, and the alternatives.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Quantifies the cost against the revenue at risk, finds the break-even churn, considers lighter options (maintenance mode, simplification, migration), plans communication and a reversible rollout, and sets a decision rule.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <em>Who uses it, and why.</em> 4% of 2 million weekly users is 80,000 people. Share of users says little by itself: what matters is who they are. Are they heavy, loyal or high-value users (suppose their retention is 92% against 70% overall)? Do they use it for a job nothing else does? Are they concentrated in a segment we care about? <em>Cost against what is at risk (invented figures).</em> At about $15,000 a month per engineer, one and a half engineers cost $22,500 a month, plus the opportunity cost of what they would otherwise build. If these users pay about $4 a month and some leave when the feature goes, the revenue at risk is the number who churn times $4. The break-even is 22,500 ÷ (80,000 × 4) = about 7.0% of those users leaving. If 5% leave, retiring saves $22,500 and loses $16,000, a net gain of $6,500 a month; if 10% leave, it loses $32,000, a net loss of $9,500; at 20%, a net loss of $41,500. So the decision turns on the churn we would cause, which I can estimate rather than guess. <em>Options between keep and kill.</em> (A) Keep as is. (B) Put it in maintenance mode: freeze features, fix only critical bugs, and cut the cost to perhaps half an engineer. (C) Simplify or fold its core into another feature. (D) Retire with a migration path, a long notice period and an export. (E) Retire outright. <em>How I'd estimate churn.</em> Look at what these users do instead when the feature is unavailable (a holdout or a past outage), survey the heaviest users, and test a deprecation notice with a small group to see who leaves. Also check dependencies: other features, integrations and support load. <em>Recommendation.</em> Start with B and measure; if the estimated churn from retirement is under about 5% and the freed capacity has a clear, better use, move to D with notice and migration. If the users are high-value and the churn estimate is above the break-even, keep it in maintenance mode and revisit when the cost or use changes. I'd decide with a rule agreed in advance and tell users plainly what is happening and why. <em>Signals.</em> Who uses it; cost against revenue at risk; a computed break-even; options between keep and kill; communication and reversibility.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c16-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C16-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C16 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What goes into a good PRD?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The requirements and the designs.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Lists the sections (problem, goals, non-goals, requirements, metrics, risks) and says why each exists.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes good from bad examples, scales length to stakes, treats the document as a living tool for alignment, and mentions the decision log and the partners.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> A PRD exists to align a team on what to build and why, so the best test of one is whether someone new could read it and explain the problem, the goal and what is out of scope. <em>Problem.</em> Who has what problem, with evidence, and the cost of leaving it. No solution in it: &quot;Shoppers who search by name get no results on 18% of queries, and 60% leave&quot; and not &quot;we need a better search.&quot; <em>Goals and metrics.</em> The outcome and how we will measure it: a primary metric with a threshold, a guardrail, and a counter metric (C05). <em>Non-goals.</em> Things a reasonable stakeholder might have expected that we are not doing in this version. They prevent scope creep and end arguments early. <em>Users and stories.</em> Who is affected, and the key scenarios as short stories with acceptance criteria that QA can test. <em>Requirements.</em> In priority order (must, should, could), each specific enough that an engineer can estimate it and a tester can test it: &quot;results load in under 1.5 seconds at the 95th percentile,&quot; not &quot;fast.&quot; <em>Risks, assumptions and open questions.</em> The riskiest assumption and the cheap test for it, what could go wrong, and what is undecided with an owner and a date. <em>Launch and measurement.</em> How it ships (staged), who decides go or no-go and on what criteria, how results will be read. I'd scale it to the stakes: two pages for a small feature, longer with a summary on top for a multi-team launch. I'd keep a decision log at the top and update the document as we learn, and I'd review it with the engineering lead and designer before anyone commits. The mistake is writing a long document that no one reads or a feature list with no problem. Those are signals that the spec is for the author, not the team.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c16-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C16-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C16 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You expected a feature to take four weeks. Engineering says twelve. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Push back and ask them to find ways to be faster,&quot; or &quot;accept it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks why, gets a range and assumptions, and looks at scope options.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses the outside view, separates complexity from risk, proposes a smaller first slice and checkpoints, and avoids pressure on the estimate.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> A threefold gap between what I expected and what engineering says is information. Either my expectation was missing something about the system, or the scope is bigger than I realized. I'd start with curiosity, not pressure. <em>Understand.</em> I'd ask for the breakdown: where does the time go, what is the riskiest part, what assumptions does 12 weeks rest on, and what is the range? Suppose it is 8 to 16 weeks, driven by a data model that does not exist yet. Then I'd learn that my four-week picture assumed reuse of something that is not there. <em>Check my side.</em> What did I assume, and from what? If I had a reason (a similar feature took four weeks), compare with this one: what differs? <em>Use the outside view.</em> How have this team's estimates compared with actuals? If their past projects ran 1.1 to 2.0 times their estimates, 12 weeks may itself be optimistic, which means planning for four is not realistic for the full scope. <em>Negotiate scope, not the number.</em> If I need something in four to six weeks, ask: &quot;What is the smallest version that delivers the core of the user's job, and how long does it take?&quot; Perhaps a first slice that handles the common case in 4 to 5 weeks, with the full version later. Or a two-day technical spike to narrow the range before we commit. <em>Decide and communicate.</em> Option 1: ship the smaller slice in five weeks and the remainder in a follow-up. Option 2: keep full scope and plan 12 to 16 weeks, with a checkpoint at week 3. I'd explain the tradeoff to stakeholders with the range and the reason. What I would not do is ask engineers to promise four weeks for the full scope. That produces hidden shortcuts and a late surprise.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c16-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C16-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C16 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Microsoft</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> You are shipping admin audit logs in an enterprise product. Legal wants 180-day retention, security wants the logs encrypted and access-controlled, finance is worried about storage cost, and support wants training. How do you coordinate?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Hold a meeting with everyone and get alignment.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Identifies what each partner needs and when, and sequences the work.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Puts numbers on the cost tradeoff, spots a conflict between requirements (retention versus erasure requests), proposes options and a decision owner, and plans a staged rollout with design partners.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I know the product only from public information; nothing here describes any real plan. I'd treat each partner as holding a constraint that is cheap to meet early and expensive late, and sequence accordingly. <em>Legal and privacy first.</em> Retention length and the handling of personal data shape the design. They may also create a conflict: logs kept 180 days for audit may contain personal data that a person can ask to have erased in some jurisdictions <code>[V: GDPR Article 17 right to erasure, unless retention is legally required, as summarized in guidance]</code>. I'd ask legal for the rule, not guess, and put the answer in the spec (for example, logs record identifiers that can be pseudonymized on request, with the audit trail intact). <em>Security.</em> Design review of how logs are written (tamper-resistant), who can read them (administrators only, with their own access logged), and encryption. Early involvement avoids a high-risk finding in the week before launch. <em>Finance.</em> Put numbers on retention. Suppose (invented) storage costs $4 per customer per month at 90 days and $8 at 180 days; for 2,000 customers that is $8,000 against $16,000 a month, $96,000 a year more. Options to put to finance and product leadership: include 90 days in all plans and sell 180 days as a higher tier; include 180 for all and absorb the cost; or tier by customer size. I'd bring the options with the cost and the demand evidence (how many customers need 180 days), not a request. <em>Support and operations.</em> Training, scripts for the questions they will receive (&quot;why can't I see events older than 90 days?&quot;), and an escalation route. Involve them two to three weeks before launch. <em>Engineering and design.</em> Confirm the event stream carries the data; design search and filters. <em>Sequence and ownership.</em> Week 1: legal, security and finance decisions with a named owner each. Weeks 2 to 6: build. Week 5: support training. Launch: staged, first to ten design-partner customers, with go or no-go criteria in the spec (no S1 defects, search within the performance target, support trained). I'd keep a decision log and a short weekly note to all partners. <em>Where I'd decide.</em> If legal and finance conflict, I'd bring the options to the shared leader with a recommendation. What would change my plan: legal saying that a particular market needs a different retention rule, which would turn retention into a regional setting. <em>Company note.</em> Microsoft is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that enterprise-product prompts involve compliance and security partners <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Microsoft data or product behavior is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c16-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C16-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C16 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your engineering lead and your designer both think your plan is wrong, for different reasons. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Overrule them&quot; or &quot;do what they say.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Listens to each, restates the shared goal, and looks for evidence.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates the criteria each is using, finds the cheapest test, names the decision owner, commits afterward, and keeps the relationships intact.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> When two of my closest partners disagree with my plan, I'd take it as a strong signal and not as an obstacle. I'd talk to each separately first, then together. <em>Listen for the criteria.</em> The engineer thinks it is too risky or too big: the criterion is cost and reliability. The designer thinks the experience will confuse users: the criterion is user value and usability. They may be seeing different parts of the same flaw. I'd ask each: &quot;What would you do instead, and what are you worried will happen if we go with my plan?&quot; <em>Restate the shared goal.</em> &quot;We all want [goal] by [date]. Do we agree on that?&quot; If there is disagreement about the goal itself, that is the real conflict and I'd resolve it first. <em>Find the cheapest evidence.</em> Is there a way to test the contested points quickly? A paper prototype tested with five users answers the designer's concern in two days; a technical spike answers the engineer's in two or three. I'd propose both and agree beforehand what result would change the plan. <em>Decide and own it.</em> If the evidence is clear, change the plan and say what I learned. If it is not, I decide, since the call on scope and priority is mine, and I explain the reasoning and the risk I am accepting, and invite them to say what would make us revisit. Then we commit: &quot;disagree and commit,&quot; with a date to review. <em>Protect the relationship.</em> I'd thank them for the challenge, credit their input in the final plan, and make sure that raising concerns early is rewarded. What I'd avoid is deciding by rank, hiding the disagreement or reopening the decision every week. What would change my mind: any evidence that the plan fails the user or cannot be built in time.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c16-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C16-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C16 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your engineers ask for a full quarter to refactor a core system. Sales wants three features. How do you decide?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Features, because they drive revenue,&quot; or &quot;refactor, because debt is dangerous.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks for the cost of the debt and compares it with the features.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes payback and the effect of risk, shows sensitivity, proposes staging with a measure, and communicates the tradeoff to sales.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd refuse to treat it as engineering against sales and ask the same question of both: what does each return for the capacity it uses? For the refactoring I need the principal, the interest, and the risk. <em>Numbers (invented).</em> Six engineers, two-week sprints: 12 person-weeks a sprint. The refactor costs 24 person-weeks (two sprints, the principal). Workarounds cost about 10% of capacity, 1.2 person-weeks per sprint (the interest). Payback on capacity alone: 24 ÷ 1.2 = 20 sprints, about 40 weeks. Over a year (26 sprints), capacity savings are 26 × 1.2 = 31.2 person-weeks, more than the 24 spent: a net of +7.2 person-weeks on time savings alone, and payback takes the first 20 sprints of the year. <em>But debt also carries risk.</em> Suppose the system has two serious incidents a year, each costing $150,000 in lost revenue and recovery, and the refactor would roughly halve them: $150,000 a year of avoided loss. At about $15,000 per person-week of engineering value (invented), the time savings are 31.2 × $15,000 = $468,000, plus $150,000 of avoided incidents, $618,000 a year, against a cost of 24 × $15,000 = $360,000. So the year-one return is about +$258,000, and over two years it is $1,236,000 against $360,000, a gain of $876,000. The case is positive in year one and strong over two, if the interest and the incident rate hold. <em>Compare with the features.</em> Size the three sales features the same way (C15.5): expected value per person-week. If they return more than $15,000 per person-week, they win this year; if less, the refactor is at least as good. <em>Sensitivity.</em> The interest and the incident rate are the uncertain numbers. If interest is 5% rather than 10%, the time savings halve to $234,000 and the year-one return is about break-even (+$24,000), and if the incident saving also disappears it is −$126,000. So I'd ask the team to measure the interest: hours lost to the system over the past quarter, and the incident history. <em>Decision.</em> Not a whole quarter of nothing else. Stage it: do the highest-interest part first, perhaps 8 person-weeks, with a measure of hours saved and incidents after, and ship the most valuable one or two sales features in the same quarter. Tell sales what they get and when, and why the refactor is part of reliability they rely on. Review at the end of the first stage. What would change my mind: a major incident in the system, which would move the whole refactor up, or a sales feature tied to a signed commitment.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c16-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C16-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C16 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A teammate suggests using an AI tool to write the PRD and summarize the user research for you. What do you think, and what is your approach?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Great, it saves time,&quot; or &quot;I wouldn't use it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Names what it is good for and what it is not, with verification.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Distinguishes drafts from evidence, protects confidential data, keeps the decision and the research with people, labels the work, and gives a concrete workflow.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> <code>[as of 2026-10]</code> I'd use it for speed on drafts and structure, and I'd be strict about what counts as evidence. A model's output is a draft or a hypothesis. It is not a source. <em>For the PRD.</em> I'd write the problem, the goal and the non-goals myself, because they are my judgment, and give the tool my bullet points to turn into a first draft, to suggest missing sections, to list edge cases and to argue against the plan (a red team). Then I'd edit hard: check every number, every claim about users and every technical statement against a source, and remove generic text that says nothing specific. <em>For the research.</em> The evidence is the participants and the transcripts (C06). I could use a tool to organize notes I give it, to propose themes and to find quotes, but I'd check each theme against the transcripts and quote only what a person actually said. A summary is a pointer to the transcripts, not a replacement for them. And a model-generated &quot;user&quot; is not a user: its answers say what text on the internet looks like, not what my customers do. <em>Data and privacy.</em> I'd follow the company's policy: no customer data, unreleased plans or personal information in tools that are not approved for them. For approved tools, I'd still strip identifiers when I can. <em>Ownership and honesty.</em> The decision, and the accountability, stay with me and the team. I'd say in the document where AI helped and not present synthesized text as findings. If the PRD contains a claim I cannot trace to a source or my own data, it comes out. <em>What I'd tell my teammate.</em> &quot;Great for drafting and for finding gaps. For anything we rely on, we verify against a source, and the research stays with real users.&quot; What would change my practice: a tool approved for confidential data and with traceable citations to the underlying sources, in which case I'd still verify the citations.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c16-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C16-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C16 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> It is the day before launch. QA finds a bug that loses user data for 0.5% of users, with a three-day fix. Leadership wants to ship on time. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Ship and fix it later,&quot; or &quot;delay.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Classifies the severity, sizes the harm and weighs the delay.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Applies a pre-set release rule, considers staging and its limits (staging still exposes some users), identifies alternatives that remove the harm without delay, and communicates clearly with a go or no-go owner.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> Data loss is an S1 under any sensible release rule: it harms users irreversibly. My starting position is no-go for a full launch until it is fixed or contained, and I'd check that the rule agreed in advance says the same (&quot;no open S1&quot;). <em>Size the harm.</em> With 200,000 users in the first month (invented), 0.5% is 1,000 people losing data. Even a staged release does not make that safe: at 5% exposure, 50 people could lose data, which I would not accept as a planned outcome. The harm is not recoverable by a patch after the fact. <em>Size the delay.</em> A three-day delay costs something real: say $15,000 a day of expected revenue and a commitment to partners (invented), $45,000 and some reputational cost. That is small beside 1,000 people's data and the trust cost. <em>Look for ways to contain without delaying.</em> (1) Can the bug be avoided by disabling the feature path that triggers it, via a feature flag, and launching the rest? (2) Can we launch to users who do not hit the affected condition, with a safe fallback? (3) Can the fix be done in less than three days, or the launch moved by one day with partial fixes verified? (4) Can we warn and offer a backup before the risky action? Any option that removes the data-loss path is acceptable; one that merely reduces it is not. <em>Decision and communication.</em> Recommend: hold the launch for the fix, or launch with the risky path disabled if that is feasible and verified. Tell leadership the facts in five lines: what the bug does, how many users it could hit, the options with cost and date, my recommendation, and the go or no-go owner (me with the engineering lead, with the sponsor informed). Tell partners and support the new date or the reduced scope. <em>Afterward.</em> Add a regression test for the case, and look at why testing found it late. What would change my mind: evidence that the affected condition is much rarer than assumed or that the data can be recovered automatically.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c17-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C17-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C17 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What does &quot;ready to launch&quot; mean to you?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;The code is done and tested.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Lists criteria across product, reliability, data, support and constraints, with a decider.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Scales the gate to the stakes, requires a rehearsed rollback and written success criteria, notes that only the decider may waive a criterion and in writing, and links readiness to a staged plan.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> Ready means a short list of criteria is met, each with an owner, and one named person has decided go. A date is not readiness. <em>The criteria.</em> Product: acceptance criteria met, defects triaged by severity, no open S1, accessibility checked. Reliability: load tested, monitoring and alerts live, and a rollback or off-switch rehearsed, because a rollback that has never been tried is a hope. Data: events logged and verified in production, a dashboard and a baseline, and success criteria written before launch. People: support trained with scripts, sales and success informed, internal and external messages ready. Constraints: legal, privacy and security sign-offs recorded and any open risk accepted in writing by a named owner. Rollout: stages, dwell times and rollback triggers agreed. <em>The decider.</em> One person, usually the PM with the engineering lead, makes the go or no-go call at a meeting a few days before the date, with the checklist in front of them and dissent recorded. <em>Scale it.</em> A copy change behind a flag needs a light gate; a payments change needs the full one. And I'd write the criteria in week one so the date does not bend them in week six. A criterion may be waived only by the decider, in writing, so the exception is visible and rare. The mistake to avoid is &quot;launch Friday&quot; as the goal. If testing finds problems on Thursday, the right move is a smaller launch or a later date, chosen by the criteria and not by the calendar.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c17-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C17-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C17 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is a feature flag, and why should a PM care?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It turns features on and off.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Explains staged exposure, fast rollback and experiments.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Covers canary and ramp use, kill switches, testing in production with a small audience, the cost (complexity, flag debt) and what a PM does with them.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> A feature flag is a switch that turns functionality on or off for chosen users without shipping a new release. The code for the feature is deployed, but who sees it is controlled by the flag. <em>Why a PM cares.</em> First, <em>staged rollout</em>: I can show a change to staff, then 1% of users, then 10%, and widen only if it behaves, which limits the blast radius. Second, <em>fast rollback</em>: if something goes wrong, switching the flag off takes seconds, compared with an hour or more to redeploy. Third, <em>experiments</em>: flags assign users to variants, so I can compare an exposed group with a control (C14). Fourth, <em>decoupling release from launch</em>: engineering can ship code when it is ready and the business can choose the moment to turn it on, with marketing and support aligned. Fifth, a <em>kill switch</em> for risky components. <em>Costs.</em> Flags add complexity: more code paths to test, and flags that stay after a launch become debt, so each flag needs an owner and a removal date. A flag is not a replacement for testing: it limits the damage of a mistake, it does not prevent it. <em>What I do with them.</em> Agree with engineering which launches use flags, who can flip them, the stages and the rollback triggers, and make sure the off-switch is tested before launch. Where a change cannot be flagged (a data migration, a hardware design), I treat it as higher risk and gate it harder.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c17-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C17-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C17 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Amazon</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A test of a checkout change shows orders up 0.4%, but the page loads 80 milliseconds slower. Do you launch?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, orders are up,&quot; or &quot;No, latency matters.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Weighs the gain against the cost of latency using the data.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Measures the product's own latency sensitivity, computes the net under stated assumptions, considers fixing latency and staged launch, segments by device, and sets a decision rule.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd not decide on the sign of each number. I'd ask whether the 0.4% survives the latency, and I'd use our own data for the cost of slowness. <em>A rough check.</em> A widely cited claim from the mid-2000s is that each additional 100 milliseconds of latency cost a retailer about 1% of sales <code>[V: Greg Linden, Make Data Useful, Stanford talk slides, 29 November 2006: 100 ms extra load time, 1 percent drop in Amazon sales; cited by Kohavi and Longbotham, 2007; the size today is not established]</code>. That figure is old and context-specific (Kohavi's own later slides describe it as pages slowed by 100 to 250 milliseconds costing about 1% of revenue, so scaling it in a straight line is itself an assumption), so I'd treat it only as a prompt for an assumption. If it held here, 80 milliseconds costs about 0.8% of orders. Net: +0.4% − 0.8% = −0.4%. On that assumption, launching loses orders. If our sensitivity were only 0.25% per 100 milliseconds, the cost is 0.2% and the net is +0.2%. So the answer depends on a number I can measure. <em>Measure the sensitivity.</em> Run an experiment that adds artificial delay to a random group (say +100 ms) and measure the change in orders. That gives our own cost per 100 milliseconds, by device (mobile users on slow connections are often more sensitive). <em>Look for a better option.</em> Why is the page slower? If the cause is fixable (an uncompressed image, a blocking script), fix it and retest: the 0.4% gain without the latency may be the real result. If the slowdown is inherent, ask whether the gain justifies it on the segments where it is positive. <em>Decision.</em> Do not launch to everyone on this readout. Fix the latency and retest, or launch in stages to the segments (desktop, fast connections) where the net is positive, with the latency guardrail watched and a holdout. The rule: launch if orders are up by more than the latency cost, with the interval for the net above zero. What would change my mind: a measured sensitivity much lower than the claim. <em>Company note.</em> Amazon is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that launch prompts weigh conversion gains against latency and reliability <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Amazon data or practice is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c17-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C17-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C17 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Two days after launch, sign-up conversion has dropped 3%. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Roll back,&quot; or &quot;investigate.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Confirms the drop is real, relates it to the launch, and decides on rollback with the cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses the pre-set triggers, applies the diagnosis steps, separates launch effect from other causes, decides with numbers, and communicates.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'd treat it as an incident with a diagnosis attached. First, is it real and is it ours? I'd apply step zero from C13: is the data complete, is 3% outside the normal swing (say daily swings are ±1.5%, so 3% is notable), and does the timing line up with the launch (the drop starts at the release hour and not before)? If the launch was staged, compare the exposed group with the unexposed: a drop only in the exposed group is strong evidence the launch is the cause; if both dropped, look for an external cause. <em>Check the trigger sheet.</em> If a rollback trigger was set (&quot;sign-up conversion down more than 3% over a rolling 24 hours&quot;), and it is hit, the action is pre-agreed: pause the ramp or turn off the flag, then diagnose. If no trigger existed, I'd decide with numbers. <em>The numbers.</em> With 10,000 sign-ups a day, a 3% drop is 300 sign-ups a day. Suppose each is worth $10 in lifetime margin (invented): $3,000 a day. The launch must have been expected to add more than that to be worth keeping. If the benefit is not yet visible, or the drop is concentrated in a segment, rollback or a partial rollback is cheap relative to waiting. <em>Locate.</em> Segment by platform, browser, country and new versus returning users (C13.5): if one browser accounts for most of the drop, the cause is likely a bug and the fix is quick. If the drop is spread evenly, it could be the design itself, and an experiment is the right tool. <em>Decide and communicate.</em> If the drop is confirmed in the exposed group and the cause is not quickly fixable, roll back or reduce exposure to 10% while diagnosing. Tell stakeholders the five lines (what, since when, what we know, what we are doing, next update). What would change my mind: the drop disappearing when the data finishes arriving.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c17-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C17-05</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C17 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A hardware accessory has a software defect found two weeks before manufacturing locks. Decide.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Delay and fix it,&quot; or &quot;ship and patch.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Recognizes irreversibility, sizes the defect and weighs options by cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Tests whether the fix-later path is real (including any re-certification), uses a gate that preserves the option to delay, quantifies expected cost, considers supply and support, and treats safety as separate.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I know the situation only in general terms; nothing here describes any real product. The first question is what kind of defect: if it can hurt someone, safety decides and it is not a tradeoff; I'll assume it is a functional defect. <em>Classify.</em> The manufactured parts are irreversible after the lock; the firmware is changeable only through an update path. So the question is whether the fix-later path is real. I'd ask three things. Can the fix be delivered over the air, to units affected by the defect? Does the fix need regulatory re-certification, and how long does that take? What is the exposure window before the fix reaches customers? <em>Numbers (invented).</em> The first run is 400,000 units. The defect affects 2.5% in lab tests: 10,000 units. A field problem costs about $90 per affected unit (support, returns, replacement): $900,000. Suppose the fix requires a three-week re-certification before it can be released. In the first three weeks, 40% of volume sells, 160,000 units, of which 2.5% are affected: 4,000 units and $360,000 of field cost in the window. Delaying the lock by 10 days would miss the main selling window and cost an estimated $1,500,000. <em>Options.</em> (A) Lock on time; start the re-certification now so the fix is ready within about three weeks; mitigate the defect in the meantime with a documented workaround and a support script; and run a gate: by day 7, verify that the fix works on affected units and that the update path reaches them. (B) Delay the lock 10 days: $1,500,000 for sure. (C) Lock on time and ship as is: $900,000 of field cost, plus the reputational cost. <em>Compare.</em> If the gate passes (say probability 0.75), expected cost is the window cost, $360,000. If it fails (0.25), I delay the lock by the remaining time at, say, $750,000. Expected: 0.75 × $360,000 + 0.25 × $750,000 = $270,000 + $187,500 = $457,500. That beats B ($1,500,000) and C ($900,000). The gate preserves the option to delay while buying the one piece of evidence that decides. <em>Also.</em> Check that the first units out can be flagged and updated before shipping; size support; and decide who may decide at the gate. What would change my mind: the defect turning out to be safety-related, or the update path failing in the lab on day 2, in which case delay immediately. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that hardware prompts at Apple include defects found near manufacturing deadlines <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any real product, process or numbers.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c17-06\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C17-06</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C17 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Your launch date is fixed and engineering says the remaining work will take three times longer than planned. Five other teams must deliver pieces. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Work harder,&quot; &quot;add people,&quot; or &quot;push the date.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Looks at scope, dependencies and the critical path, and negotiates scope.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Computes compounding dependency risk, orders scope cuts in advance, preserves the launch criteria, proposes a staged launch on the date, and communicates early with options.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> With a fixed date and a threefold gap, the lever is scope or exposure, not effort. I'd do four things this week. <em>Understand the gap.</em> Ask engineering for the range and the riskiest part (C16.4), and check against the history of similar projects. Separate what is truly three times longer from what can be descoped. <em>Map the dependencies and compound them.</em> Five other teams, each, say, 90% likely to deliver on time: 0.9⁵ = 59%. Even before the threefold estimate, the plan had a 41% chance of slipping on dependencies. I'd find the critical path, raise the weakest items (build against mocks, agree fallbacks, book reviews now), and cut the count on the path. <em>Decide the order of cuts, in advance.</em> First polish and nice-to-haves; then secondary markets and features; never the launch criteria (quality bar, safety, accessibility, privacy). The smallest launch that delivers the core of the user's job, perhaps a third of the scope, becomes the plan for the date. The rest follows in a second release with a date. <em>Use staging.</em> Launch on the date to a smaller group or one market, with the remaining scope behind flags to be enabled as it is ready. That meets the date in a meaningful way without exposing everyone to unfinished work. <em>Communicate.</em> Go to the sponsor now with options and costs, not on the eve of the date: &quot;At full scope we will miss by about X weeks; for the date we can ship the core to one market; adding people helps little at this stage (the arithmetic from C15.4); here is my recommendation.&quot; What I'd avoid: promising the full scope on the date, asking engineers to cut testing, or hoping. What would change my mind: a dependency owner offering to pull in their delivery.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c17-07\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C17-07</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> execution · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Hard · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C17 · <strong>Competency:</strong> EX · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> It is 2 a.m. You are the PM on call. An alert shows that 5% of users cannot complete a core action since last evening's release. What do you do?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Wake up the engineers and fix it.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Confirms impact, contains by rollback, communicates, then diagnoses.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Uses pre-agreed triggers and decision rights, runs the PM's parallel tracks (impact, communication, decisions, log), weighs rollback against fix forward, and sets up a blameless postmortem.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> My job is not to debug; it is to make sure the right people are working the problem, the impact is limited, and everyone who needs to know does. <em>First 15 minutes (FW-C17-06).</em> (1) <em>Confirm impact:</em> who, how many, since when, how severe? Is the alert real (a second source)? Five percent of users failing at a core action is severe. (2) <em>Contain:</em> the release was last evening; if it was behind a flag, the on-call engineer can turn it off, and the trigger sheet probably says to. I'd confirm that and not debate. If it cannot be flagged, decide quickly between a rollback and a fix forward: rollback is usually safer if the previous version is known good and the rollback is rehearsed. (3) <em>Communicate:</em> an internal status in the incident channel with the five lines, and a plan for the customer message and the status page if it will last past the morning. Tell support what to say. (4) <em>Decide the tradeoffs the engineers bring:</em> for example, disabling the new feature entirely for 100% of users or only for the affected 5%; the PM is the person who weighs customer impact and scope. (5) <em>Log:</em> keep a timeline. <em>Protect the team.</em> Provide one voice upward with a status every 30 to 60 minutes, so the engineers working the fix are not interrupted by questions. <em>After containment.</em> Diagnose with the C13 sequence (what changed, who is affected, timing, location, size), confirm that metrics recover, and decide whether to relaunch with a fix and a smaller ramp. <em>Postmortem.</em> Within a few days, a blameless review: timeline, root causes, why testing and monitoring did not catch it earlier, what went well, and actions with owners and dates (for example, an alert on the core action's success rate, a canary stage). I'd own the product-impact and decision sections and follow up the actions. What would change my mind: if the rollback itself is risky (a data change), in which case a targeted disable and fix forward may be better, and I'd say so with the reasoning.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c18-01\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C18-01</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C18 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> What is strategy, and how is it different from a plan or a goal?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;It's the long-term plan to achieve goals.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Defines strategy as choices based on a diagnosis, distinct from goals and plans.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Gives the kernel (diagnosis, guiding policy, coherent actions), includes what is declined, and offers a short example and a test for bad strategy.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> A goal is where you want to end up. A plan is a list of steps and dates. A strategy is the logic that connects them: a diagnosis of the situation, an approach chosen to deal with it, and actions that reinforce each other. The distinguishing feature is choice, and especially what you decide not to do. An example. A clinic-scheduling product says: &quot;Grow 40% and launch mobile, analytics, integrations and an AI assistant.&quot; That is a goal and a project list. A strategy would read: &quot;Diagnosis: 70% of our revenue comes from small clinics, who churn in the first 90 days because setup is hard, while large clinics need integrations we cannot deliver. Policy: win small clinics by being the easiest to set up; decline large-clinic integrations and new markets this year. Actions: guided setup, a calendar import tool, referral through clinic associations, one simple price.&quot; The actions all push in the same direction, and the declined items are named. A quick test for a real strategy: can you say what the company will not do, and why? If every initiative fits, it is a wish list. Another test: would the plan look the same under any strategy? Then it expresses none. At my level, strategy shows up as understanding the company's choices and connecting my product's decisions to them, and as the ability to reason about a strategic question when it arises.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c18-02\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C18-02</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Beginner · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C18 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> —</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Estimate the size of the market for a dog-walking app in a city of 1.2 million households.</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> One estimate with no check.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Two routes (top-down, bottom-up), a range, and a so-what.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Explains the gap between routes, checks against a reality constraint, separates total from obtainable, and states the swing assumption and a way to test it.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> I'll estimate two ways and compare. <em>Top-down.</em> 1,200,000 households; suppose 25% own a dog: 300,000. Say 10% use a paid walker regularly: 30,000 households. At 3 walks a week for 52 weeks at $20 a walk: 30,000 × 3 × 52 × $20 = $93.6 million a year. <em>Bottom-up (supply side).</em> Suppose 2,000 active walkers in the city, each doing 6 walks a day, 5 days a week, 50 weeks a year, at $20: 2,000 × 6 × 5 × 50 × $20 = $60 million a year. <em>Compare.</em> The top-down is 1.6 times the bottom-up. A likely reason: the top-down adoption (10% of owners using a walker, 3 walks a week) is optimistic, while the bottom-up counts only walkers who are currently active, and informal walkers (neighbors, friends) are not counted in either. So the current paid market is probably nearer the lower figure. <em>Reality check.</em> A walker doing 6 walks a day, 5 days a week, earns $600 a week, about $30,000 a year: plausible for a full-time walker. Cross-check demand against supply: 30,000 households at 3 walks a week is 90,000 walks a week; at 30 walks per walker per week that needs 3,000 walkers, 1.5 times the 2,000 assumed. That is the same 1.5 gap, so the demand assumptions (adoption and frequency) are the likelier overstatement. <em>Range and so-what.</em> $60 to $94 million for all paid walking; I'd plan on $60 million. Our obtainable share is the question: with a 5% share in three years, about $3 million in gross bookings; an app taking a 20% commission would earn about $600,000 in revenue, which may not support a team. So the size sets up the real question: what commission and share, and whether the app is more than a directory. The assumption that swings it is paid-walker adoption (10% could be 4% or 15%), which a small survey or a pilot could test.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c18-03\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C18-03</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C18 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Apple</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> Should a device maker launch a subscription bundle of its services?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, bundles increase revenue,&quot; or &quot;no, it will cannibalize.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Diagnoses the situation, models the economics, and addresses cannibalization.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Names ecosystem integration as the source of advantage, computes the break-even on cannibalization and the retention benefit, states the assumption to test, and proposes a staged test.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general: I know the product only from public information, and nothing here describes any real plan. I'd first ask what the company wants (more revenue per customer, lower churn, a stronger ecosystem) and diagnose the current state: how many customers pay for how many services. <em>Diagnosis (invented).</em> Per 1,000 users: 250 pay for two or more services (average $22 a month), 450 pay for one ($8), and 300 pay for none. A bundle of four services at $20 is cheaper than buying them separately. <em>Where the advantage comes from.</em> Ecosystem integration: the services work better together and with the device, and customers who use several are less likely to leave. A bundle reinforces that and makes switching costlier (C18.5). <em>Economics.</em> Three effects. <em>Cannibalization:</em> the 250 multi-service payers move to $20 and lose $2 each: −$500 a month per 1,000 users. <em>Upgrades:</em> single-service payers who add the others at +$12 each; suppose 5% do: 450 × 0.05 × $12 = +$270. <em>New payers:</em> non-payers who join at $20; suppose 3%: 300 × 0.03 × $20 = +$180. Net: −$500 + $270 + $180 = −$50 a month per 1,000 users: roughly break-even on the first month. With 8% upgrades and 4% new payers: +$172. So on revenue alone the bundle is marginal; its value depends on adoption. <em>The retention benefit.</em> If bundle subscribers churn less, say 2.1% a month against 3.0% (invented), the expected lifetime rises from 33 months to 48 months. For a multi-service payer, $22 a month over 33 months is $733; $20 over 48 months is $952: a gain of about $219 per customer in lifetime revenue, in spite of the lower monthly price. So the bundle pays if the retention effect is real, and it does not if it is not. That is the assumption to test. <em>Other effects.</em> Pricing power: a bundle makes it harder to raise individual prices; each service's team may lose visibility of its own economics; regulators may examine bundles that tie services together <code>[V: the European Commission's 2022 decision on Amazon's Prime and Buy Box practices is an example]</code>. <em>Recommendation.</em> Test it before committing: offer the bundle in one market for a quarter, with a holdout, and measure upgrades, new payers and, most importantly, retention of bundle subscribers against a matched group. Launch more widely if retention improves by enough to give a positive lifetime value (at least a 20% longer lifetime in this example). I would change my mind if the retention gain were small, in which case the bundle is a discount. <em>Company note.</em> Apple is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from third-party reports that strategy prompts at Apple include ecosystem and services decisions <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No claim is made about any real bundle, price or data.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c18-04\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C18-04</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Type:</strong> strategy · <strong>Difficulty:</strong> Intermediate · <strong>Source chapter:</strong> C18 · <strong>Competency:</strong> ST · <strong>Company tag:</strong> Google</li>\n<li><strong>Prompt:</strong> A company has a popular free, ad-supported consumer product. Should it launch a paid, ad-free tier?</li>\n<li><strong>Rubric</strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, subscriptions are good revenue.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Computes the revenue gained against ad revenue lost, and notes who would pay.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Recognizes selection (heavy users are worth more in ads), sets the break-even, considers the effects on the free product, positioning and trust, and proposes a test.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Model answer:</strong> The setting is general; I know such products only from public information, and nothing here describes a real plan. The crux: the people who would pay to remove ads are the people the ads earn the most from. <em>Numbers (invented).</em> 1,000,000 monthly users; average ad revenue $2.40 per user per month. A paid tier at $8. Suppose 4% convert: 40,000 payers, $320,000 a month. If the converters were average users, the ad revenue lost is 40,000 × $2.40 = $96,000, a net of +$224,000. But paying users are typically heavier users: if their ad value is $5 a month, the loss is $200,000, a net of +$120,000. The break-even is where the converters' ad value equals the price: if they are worth $8 a month in ads, the tier gains nothing. So the key estimate is the ad value of the converters, which I can measure from usage. <em>Other effects.</em> Does the paid tier change the free product (fewer heavy users sharing content, a weaker network)? Does it affect advertisers (a smaller audience)? Does it change how users see the company (a paid product has to be better; trust is higher when the customer pays)? Is there a segment (professionals) that would pay for features beyond ad removal, which would justify the price better? <em>Alternatives.</em> A lower-priced tier; feature-based paid tiers; a usage limit on the free tier. I'd evaluate the paid tier on the heavy segments first. <em>Recommendation.</em> Run a limited test: offer the paid tier to a sample of heavy users, measure conversion, the ad revenue they leave, retention and any effect on others, and compute the net per converter. Launch if the net per converter is clearly positive and retention is higher among payers. What would change my mind: converters' ad value near $8. <em>Company note.</em> Google is the setting only: <code>Inferred</code> from candidate reports that strategy prompts at Google concern monetizing free consumer products <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. No Google data or plan is claimed.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"app-e-iq-c18-05\"></span>\n<h3>IQ-C18-05</h3>\n";
